# qu_multisine_acceleration.qu — the same multisine, several ways to
# compute it, and what "accelerated" actually buys you here.
#
# Five ways exist to compute this exact workload; this file runs the two
# that matter for a script you'd actually write (baseline loop vs. full
# vectorization) and documents all five so the choice is informed, not
# guessed:
#
#   [1] BASELINE (unaccelerated) — a scalar `for` loop over the 51 tones,
#       each iteration vectorized over the N-length time axis. Run below.
#       Qu has no reasonable scalar-loop-over-N alternative, so this IS
#       the right "unaccelerated" reference, not a strawman.
#   [2] FULL VECTORIZATION (accelerated) — zero explicit loops: reshape
#       into an (N,1) time column and a (1,K) frequency row, outer-product
#       them into an (N,K) phase matrix via broadcast multiply, `sin()`
#       it, then collapse to (N,1) with one matmul against the amplitude
#       vector. Run below, ~1.6-1.9x faster than [1] on a 16-core desktop.
#   [3] `parallel for` (accelerated, usually) — disjoint per-tone writes
#       into a shared (N,K) matrix, summed after the loop closes. NOT run
#       here: at this problem's size the one large shared output matrix
#       hits a real copy-on-write perf trap (see benchmarks/
#       multisine_acceleration/README.md's "parallel for and reductions"
#       section) and comes out ~30x SLOWER than [1] — a genuine, measured
#       result, not a demo simplification. `parallel for` is NOT slow in
#       general (the same directory's parallel_isolation.qu shows a clean
#       ~4.7x speedup on a workload with no large shared buffer); it is
#       slow specifically for a multisine synthesized this way.
#   [4] pool + queue + workers (accelerated) — split the 51 tones into 8
#       chunks, each a queued job on a worker pool, partial sums combined
#       after `run jobs on pool(...)` returns. NOT run here for brevity;
#       tied for fastest of all five methods in the referenced benchmark,
#       because no method-3-style shared mutable buffer is ever touched.
#   [5] `gpu_matmul` (accelerated, sometimes) — method [2]'s own basis
#       matrix collapsed on the GPU instead of the CPU. NOT run here: it
#       needs a `qu.exe` built with `--features gpu`, off by default. Also
#       the one genuine surprise: ~3x SLOWER than [1] on this workload,
#       because a "skinny" (N,51)x(51,1) multiply is dominated by
#       upload/dispatch/readback overhead, not by the raw FLOP count GPUs
#       are good at — see the benchmark README's "GPU" section.
#
# `benchmarks/multisine_acceleration/bench.qu` runs and cross-checks all
# five in one process, plus a full write-up of what changed the timing
# results (`benchmarks/multisine_acceleration/README.md`).
#
# Where does `backend auto` (line below) fit into this? It's a different
# axis entirely: not "which of the 5 algorithms did YOU write", but "for
# each individual operation (matmul, FFT, elementwise) the RUNTIME already
# does, which physical backend runs it" — CPU vs. GPU vs. a specific
# vendor library, decided per call by measured speed on representative
# workloads (see spec §20), not a fixed compile-time choice. Methods [1]
# and [2] below both run under `backend auto`; it does not turn one into
# the other; it only affects how the arithmetic THEY already specify gets
# dispatched underneath.
#
# The workload: x(t) = sum_{k=1..51} A_k * sin(2*pi*f_k*t + phi_k),
# Fs = 5000 Hz, duration = 300 s (N = 1,500,000 samples). 51 tones
# log-spaced from 5 Hz to 2000 Hz (comfortably under Nyquist), unit-ish
# RMS amplitude, phases drawn from a fixed seed so the signal is
# reproducible run to run.
#
# Construct exercised (spec section -> use):
#   §12  logspace, ranges, reshape
#   §21  seed()/rand() for reproducible phases
#   §16  for loop, vectorized per-tone (method [1])
#   §14  broadcasting, outer products via matmul (method [2])
#   §24  rms, crest factor via max/abs

backend auto

#%% Setup
seed(2026)

Fs = 5000                      # sample rate (Hz)
duration = 300                 # record length (s)
N = round(Fs * duration)       # 1,500,000 samples
K = 51                         # number of tones

dt = 1 / Fs
t = linspace(0, (N - 1) * dt, N)   # N-length time vector -- plain `to`/`step`
                                    # ranges cap out at 1,000,000 elements
                                    # (qu-core::DEFAULT_ELEMENT_LIMIT), so
                                    # `linspace`, which has no such cap, is
                                    # used here to reach N = 1,500,000

f   = logspace(log10(5), log10(2000), K)   # 51 tones, 5 Hz .. 2000 Hz
A   = ones(K) / sqrt(K)                    # unit-ish RMS amplitude budget
phi = rand(K) * 2 * pi                     # reproducible random phases

print("N = {N} samples, K = {K} tones, Fs = {Fs} Hz, duration = {duration} s")

#%% [1] Baseline (unaccelerated) -- loop over tones, vectorized per tone over N
#
# `mem_usage()` reports this process's RESIDENT set (pages actually
# mapped in), not what was merely requested -- `zeros(N)` alone barely
# moves it (the OS backs a fresh zero-filled allocation with a shared
# zero page until something writes to it), so the delta below is taken
# AFTER x is filled with real values, when the pages are genuinely
# touched and the number means something.
mem0 = mem_usage()
tic()
x = zeros(N)
for k = 0 to K - 1
    x = x + A[k] * sin(2 * pi * f[k] * t + phi[k])
end for
t_baseline = toc()
mem1 = mem_usage()

print("[1] baseline loop      : {t_baseline:.4f} s")
print("rms(x)   = {rms(x):.6f}")
print("crest(x) = {max(abs(x)) / rms(x):.4f}")

#%% [2] Full vectorization (accelerated) -- one broadcast/matmul expression
tic()
T_col   = reshape(t, N, 1)
F_row   = reshape(f, 1, K)
Phi_row = reshape(phi, 1, K)
A_col   = reshape(A, K, 1)

Phase = 2 * pi * (T_col * F_row)    # (N,1) x (1,K) -> (N,K) outer product
Basis = sin(Phase + Phi_row)        # (N,K), phi broadcast across all N rows
x2    = (Basis * A_col)[:, 0]       # (N,K) x (K,1) -> (N,1), flattened to N
t_vectorized = toc()
mem2 = mem_usage()

print("[2] full vectorization : {t_vectorized:.4f} s  ({t_baseline / t_vectorized:.2f}x vs [1])")
print("max|x - x2| = {max(abs(x - x2)):.3e}  (both methods, same signal)")

#%% Speed AND memory, side by side -- timing alone hides the tradeoff
#
# [1] holds only N-length arrays (x, t, and one tone's worth of scratch
# per iteration): O(N) memory. [2] builds an explicit (N,K) `Basis`
# matrix -- N=1,500,000 x K=51 doubles, ~612 MB -- to buy its speedup:
# O(N*K) memory. Faster is not automatically lighter; here it is the
# opposite, and only measuring both numbers shows that.
mb1 = (mem1 - mem0) / 1e6
mb2 = (mem2 - mem1) / 1e6
basis_mb = numel(Basis) * 8 / 1e6
print("")
print("           time (s)   RSS delta (MB)")
print("[1] baseline    {t_baseline:8.4f}   {mb1:8.1f}")
print("[2] vectorized  {t_vectorized:8.4f}   {mb2:8.1f}   ({basis_mb:.0f} MB is Basis alone)")

#%% Plot (first 0.05 s only -- 1.5M points is too dense to eyeball otherwise)
n_show = round(0.05 * Fs)
plot(t[0:n_show - 1], x[0:n_show - 1])
xlabel("Time (s)")
ylabel("Amplitude")
title("Multisine, K={K} tones (first 0.05 s)")
grid on
box on
show plot
